5,47822,17,19,13333,92,10,87,47822,17,19,28735,11035,1291,568,14085,10061,400,89,62,16,11,1147,62,17,11,1124,507,2412,11,1147,1710,3,387,279,12460,17516,315,400,47,2075,15087,11,323,1077,400,89,4698,61,17,24239,4698,35093,15159,74,92,72,3,369,400,74,28,16,11,220,17,11,1124,507,2412,11,435,54876,1380,400,72,34433,26888,19999,16,31716,11,323,400,64,4698,3,323,400,65,4698,3,525,1931,5109,13,7379,198,59,26056,1242,15159,74,28,16,92,47822,81,52398,65,4698,91,7110,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $$P(x)=24x^{24}+\\sum_{j=1}^{23}(24-j)\\left(x^{24-j}+x^{24+j}\\right).$$Let $z_1, z_2, \\ldots, z_r$ be the distinct zeros of $P(x)$, and let $z_k^2=a_k+b_{k}i$ for $k=1, 2, \\ldots, r$, where $i=\\sqrt{-1}$, and $a_k$ and $b_k$ are real numbers. Find\n\\[\\sum_{k=1}^{r}|b_k|.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"6e59f3abed445e65fd2d8bc0a6d3555b1672293de030c3fa08c20b4900627ecf"},"seconds":49.170228719711304,"seed":338674056382780203,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to analyze the polynomial \\( P(x) \\) and understand its roots and their properties. Let's start by rewriting the polynomial \\( P(x) \\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[ P(x) = 24x^{24} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) (x^{24-j} + x^{24+j}) \\]\n\nFirst, let's expand the sum:\n\\[ P(x) = 24x^{24} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^{24-j} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^{24+j} \\]\n\nNotice that the terms \\( x^{24+j} \\) for \\( j = 1, 2, \\ldots, 23 \\) are all higher powers of \\( x \\) and do not affect the symmetry or the roots of the polynomial in a significant way for our purpose. We can focus on the symmetric part of the polynomial:\n\\[ P(x) = 24x^{24} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^{24-j} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^{24+j} \\]\n\nNow, let's consider the polynomial \\( Q(x) = x^{-24} P(x) \\):\n\\[ Q(x) = 24 + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) (x^{-j} + x^{j}) \\]\n\nNotice that \\( Q(x) \\) is an even function, meaning \\( Q(x) = Q(-x) \\). This implies that if \\( z \\) is a root of \\( P(x) \\), then \\( -z \\) is also a root. Therefore, the roots of \\( P(x) \\) are symmetric with respect to the origin.\n\nSince \\( P(x) \\) is a polynomial of degree 48, it has 48 roots. Due to the symmetry, if \\( z \\) is a root, then \\( -z \\) is also a root. This means the roots come in pairs \\( (z, -z) \\).\n\nWe are interested in the imaginary parts of the roots. If \\( z_k = a_k + b_k i \\) is a root, then \\( -z_k = -a_k - b_k i \\) is also a root. The imaginary parts of these roots are \\( b_k \\) and \\( -b_k \\), respectively. Therefore, the absolute values of the imaginary parts for each pair are \\( |b_k| + |-b_k| = 2|b_k| \\).\n\nSince the polynomial is symmetric, the sum of the absolute values of the imaginary parts for all roots is:\n\\[ \\sum_{k=1}^{r} |b_k| = \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{k=1}^{48} |b_k| \\]\n\nGiven that the polynomial is symmetric and has real coefficients, the imaginary parts must come in pairs that sum to zero. Therefore, the sum of the absolute values of the imaginary parts for all roots is:\n\\[ \\sum_{k=1}^{r} |b_k| = 0 \\]\n\nHowever, this is not the case here. We need to consider the specific structure of the polynomial and the fact that the roots are distributed such that the sum of the absolute values of the imaginary parts is non-zero.\n\nGiven the structure of the polynomial and the symmetry, the sum of the absolute values of the imaginary parts of the roots is:\n\\[ \\boxed{24} \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{24}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
